00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.840
PROFESSOR: So the first
thing we're going to do,

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.520
is a power module.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.439
And it's important
that when you're

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.380
trying to get draw
power from any source,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.400
even a battery or anything
like that, you want

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.380
to draw the maximum
amount of power,

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.790
you need to have the
right load on it.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.030
So we're going to try to
figure out, experimentally,

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.730
what the right load is
for this solar panel.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:57.940
And the way you do that, is you
up a resistor or potentiometer

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.993
in a closed circuit around this.

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:01.660
First, you measure
the resistance of it.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.970
Then you hook this up
in a closed circuit

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.520
and measure the
voltage drop across it.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.350
And you can calculate power
from v squared over r.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.680
And so you just do that for a
bunch of different resistances,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:15.670
just changing this.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:19.000
And you should be able to get
a graph that goes up and then

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.450
down if you graph power
versus resistance.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.250
And based off of that,
you can figure out

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.190
what the appropriate
load to place on it is.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:31.090
And so we'll generate
a power curve for that.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.370
Again, make sure you don't
measure the resistance when

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.030
it's hooked up because you're
going to fry the voltmeter.

00:01:41.153 --> 00:01:43.320
And then the second thing
we're going to have to do,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.870
is optimize the angle.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.440
So you want to put the solar
panel at different angles,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.590
both in this direction
and in this direction,

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.410
and measure, for a
variety of angles,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.750
what the power output it is.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.570
And so for that, you can
just choose a resistance

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.270
with the potentiometer
and then measure voltage

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.780
and get power that way.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.130
And then I have
protractors somewhere.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.075
Yeah, or what?

00:02:18.075 --> 00:02:18.700
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.200
PROFESSOR: Close enough, yeah.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.617
And so you can use
that to measure

00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:23.200
the angle of the solar panel.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.480
And when you're doing
this, I want you to take,

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.730
for the angle ones
and the shading ones,

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.640
I want you to take at
least 10 data points.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.110
And for the voltage
one, I want you

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.125
to take at least 15 data points
for different resistances

00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:36.625
and powers.

00:02:36.625 --> 00:02:39.220
And then the last thing
we're going to look at,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.750
is how shading the solar
panel affects the energy being

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:44.750
outputted by it.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.983
In this case, I want you to--

00:02:46.983 --> 00:02:48.400
if you look at
these solar panels,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.250
each of these long things
is an individual cell,

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:52.810
and they're wired in series.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.723
So I want you to shade the
solar panel going down this way,

00:02:56.723 --> 00:02:58.390
and then going across
this way, and just

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.930
measure the surface area
of the exposed solar panel.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.260
And then, for that one, just
choose the angle that you like,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.860
and choose a resistance
that you like,

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.370
and then measure power
being outputted by that.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.650
As you can see, it's kind
of annoying to attach wires

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.270
to other wires in
the developing world,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.110
especially if you don't
have soldering techniques

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.190
or anything like that,
easily available.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.340
So there are a lot
of different ways

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.570
of attaching them together.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.880
A lot of times, the way
you'll see people wire things,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.500
is just taking two wires
and twisting them together.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.650
And it works, as a
short-term solution,

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.880
but you'll see entire
systems rigged up that way,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.690
and yeah, it's not
particularly good.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.000
To-- are you all comfortable
calculating power given this?

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.490
Where you just do
v squared over r?

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:17.579
AUDIENCE: 19.5.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.620
PROFESSOR: We're not going.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.060
Yeah, we're not
going to use this.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.980
AUDIENCE: Goes to
500. so go up to 20.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:23.480
go up by.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:23.980
20.

00:04:27.852 --> 00:04:29.060
Yeah, it's gone up to 20 now.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.380
PROFESSOR: Base solar
panel outputs 50 watts,

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.320
and most of the tiny
resistors you see

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.995
are rated for a quarter
watt or a half watt.

00:04:46.995 --> 00:04:49.370
So if you hook them up to
this, they would just burn out.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.730
They get super hot and burn out.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:58.380
So this one's rated for, I
think like 0.3 or 0.4 amps.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.420
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.210
PROFESSOR: So change your
angle relative to pointing

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.275
to the sun.

00:05:03.275 --> 00:05:04.830
Did that make sense?

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.530
AUDIENCE: You mean
like, yeah, so--

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.947
PROFESSOR: So if
the sun is there,

00:05:07.947 --> 00:05:10.130
then tilt your
panel here and then

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:14.270
angle it towards the
sun kind of like that.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.880
Does that make sense?

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:17.810
Like the sun is there.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.757
AUDIENCE: For a couple of hours.

00:05:22.757 --> 00:05:23.590
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:24.298
Just pull it out.

00:05:24.298 --> 00:05:26.340
PROFESSOR: You might want
to move the battery out

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:29.210
of the way, too because I
think it might be shading.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.630
AUDIENCE: We're going to use
it for an angle really quick.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.450
PROFESSOR: Makes sense.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.320
AUDIENCE: Ready?

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:36.514
Yes, we're good.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.810
19.5.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:47.940
Didn't you move it?

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.485
I did move it.

00:05:49.485 --> 00:05:50.485
The [INAUDIBLE] changed.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:56.500
OK.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.420
And so now we flip it
90 degrees and then do

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.590
the exact same thing.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.120
That's what I was
trying to figure out.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.490
If they're going to go metric.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.285
You say it's 95?

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.310
PROFESSOR: This lab
is just for fun.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.550
So I gave people the option of
either making something cool

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.350
if you're feeling
creative, or just doing

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.272
each operation with
no intent other

00:06:40.272 --> 00:06:42.730
than learning the operation if
you're not feeling creative.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:43.300
It's up to you.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:44.800
So some people may
really cool stuff

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.510
and some people just had fun.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:47.960
And anything is fine today.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.300
So we're going to go
through a bunch of things.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.845
So one, is drilling
holes in sheet metal.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.800
And you'll also want
to cut the pvc pipe.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:13.610
And you could cut
this on the band saw,

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.460
but it's probably going
to be a little uneven.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.220
And this is actually faster
and really successful.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.490
So what you do with
this PVC cutter, is just

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:27.943
open it all the way up, and then
you just keep on ratcheting.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:37.620
And you fairly
quickly, cut a ring.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.600
Very handy.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.050
You couldn't use that tool
on metal, unfortunately.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:47.580
So we have this tool
for cutting pipe.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:48.920
This is the sharp bit.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.750
And these are just bearings
that allow the pipe to spin

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:52.780
around and around.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.930
So what you do is,
you open it up.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:57.700
You load the copper
in, tighten it.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.670
And then you spin it
around a few times.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.340
You tighten it,
spin, tighten, spin.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:04.710
The more times you
tighten and spin,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.420
the cleaner edge you'll get.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.780
If you really just use a lot
of force, of course it'll work,

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:10.710
but it'll be ugly.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.800
So this is copper,
which is expensive

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.300
and we don't have
that much, so this

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.330
is the one area where you need
to just cut a little ring.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.620
Don't cut half for yourself.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.180
We also are going to cut rebar.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.210
So the hacksaw is right here.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.958
For cutting rebar
while you could just

00:08:28.958 --> 00:08:31.500
hold the rebar in one hand, and
use the hacksaw in the other.

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.780
It's going to be really
miserable and near

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.380
impossible to do successfully.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:36.780
So we have a vise.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:38.640
So you can load the
rebar in and then cut.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.600
And then when sparks fly,
give it a half a second,

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:48.790
then let go.

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:49.290
Yep.

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:49.905
So you--

00:08:49.905 --> 00:08:50.745
AUDIENCE: Held it too long.

00:08:50.745 --> 00:08:51.540
PROFESSOR: Held it too long.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:52.915
And you can tell
by the diameter.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.275
AUDIENCE: Maybe with a
bigger pipe, it'll be easier.

00:09:00.275 --> 00:09:03.490
PROFESSOR: That helps
but the radius is wrong.

